{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8c409ac4136724e7", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# AudioToolAgent: An Agentic Framework for Audio-Language Models\narXiv: 2510.02995\nAuthors: Gijs Wijngaard, Elia Formisano, Michel Dumontier, Jenia Jitsev\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: speech_agent\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. A reproducible, agent-based coordination framework with modular tool adapters that achieves state-of-the-art performance on audio understanding benchmarks without requiring additional training data or fine-tuning.\n  2. A controlled empirical study across three benchmarks using Shapley-based contribution analysis to establish a measurement methodology for evaluating and swapping tools in tool-using audio systems.\n\nTags: Agent/级联式, Agent/理解型LALM, Agent/Action", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "56329db5-c377-41fc-aa2c-a3444e3b2bbc", "uuid": "c51b854c-be09-4de9-851e-1fc671da661a", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T09:15:53.447Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# AudioToolAgent: An Agentic Framework for Audio-Language Models\narXiv: 2510.02995\nAuthors: Gijs Wijngaard, Elia Formisano, Michel Dumontier, Jenia Jitsev\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: speech_agent\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. A reproducible, agent-based coordination framework with modular tool adapters that achieves state-of-the-art performance on audio understanding benchmarks without requiring additional training data or fine-tuning.\n  2. A controlled empirical study across three benchmarks using Shapley-based contribution analysis to establish a measurement methodology for evaluating and swapping tools in tool-using audio systems.\n\nTags: Agent/级联式, Agent/理解型LALM, Agent/Action", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9d6df9ab678e357f", "type": "tool_result", "content": " Commonly used speech corpora inadequately challenge academic and commercial ASR systems. In particular, speech corpora lack metadata needed for detailed analysis and WER measurement. In response, we present Earnings-21, a 39-hour corpus of earnings calls containing entity-dense speech from nine different financial sectors. This corpus is intended to benchmark ASR systems in the wild with special attention towards named entity recognition. We benchmark four commercial ASR models, two internal models built with open-source tools, and an open-source LibriSpeech model and discuss their differences in performance on Earnings-21. Using our recently released fstalign tool, we provide a candid analysis of each model&#39;s recognition capabilities under different partitions. Our analysis finds that ASR accuracy for certain NER categories is poor, presenting a significant impediment to transcript comprehension and usage. Earnings-21 bridges academic and commercial ASR system evaluation and enables further research on entity modeling and WER on real world audio.", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "56329db5-c377-41fc-aa2c-a3444e3b2bbc", "uuid": "d33fbcfb-ee37-48cd-bf27-5d6742f0b913", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T09:15:53.907Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": " Commonly used speech corpora inadequately challenge academic and commercial ASR systems. In particular, speech corpora lack metadata needed for detailed analysis and WER measurement. In response, we present Earnings-21, a 39-hour corpus of earnings calls containing entity-dense speech from nine different financial sectors. This corpus is intended to benchmark ASR systems in the wild with special attention towards named entity recognition. We benchmark four commercial ASR models, two internal models built with open-source tools, and an open-source LibriSpeech model and discuss their differences in performance on Earnings-21. Using our recently released fstalign tool, we provide a candid analysis of each model&#39;s recognition capabilities under different partitions. Our analysis finds that ASR accuracy for certain NER categories is poor, presenting a significant impediment to transcript comprehension and usage. Earnings-21 bridges academic and commercial ASR system evaluation and enables further research on entity modeling and WER on real world audio.", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a873456f3d8dffce", "type": "tool_result", "content": " Learning-based control has recently shown great efficacy in performing complex tasks for various applications. However, to deploy it in real systems, it is of vital importance to guarantee the system will stay safe. Control Barrier Functions (CBFs) offer mathematical tools for designing safety-preserving controllers for systems with known dynamics. In this article, we first introduce a model-uncertainty-aware reformulation of CBF-based safety-critical controllers using Gaussian Process (GP) regression to close the gap between an approximate mathematical model and the real system, which results in a second-order cone program (SOCP)-based control design. We then present the pointwise feasibility conditions of the resulting safety controller, highlighting the level of richness that the available system information must meet to ensure safety. We use these conditions to devise an event-triggered online data collection strategy that ensures the recursive feasibility of the learned safety controller. Our method works by constantly reasoning about whether the current information is sufficient to ensure safety or if new measurements under active safe exploration are required to reduce the uncertainty. As a result, our proposed framework can guarantee the forward invariance of the safe set defined by the CBF with high probability, even if it contains a priori unexplored regions. We validate the proposed framework in two numerical simulation experiments.", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "56329db5-c377-41fc-aa2c-a3444e3b2bbc", "uuid": "25229bc5-f251-4725-bf74-57cce636c4fc", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T09:15:54.386Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": " Learning-based control has recently shown great efficacy in performing complex tasks for various applications. However, to deploy it in real systems, it is of vital importance to guarantee the system will stay safe. Control Barrier Functions (CBFs) offer mathematical tools for designing safety-preserving controllers for systems with known dynamics. In this article, we first introduce a model-uncertainty-aware reformulation of CBF-based safety-critical controllers using Gaussian Process (GP) regression to close the gap between an approximate mathematical model and the real system, which results in a second-order cone program (SOCP)-based control design. We then present the pointwise feasibility conditions of the resulting safety controller, highlighting the level of richness that the available system information must meet to ensure safety. We use these conditions to devise an event-triggered online data collection strategy that ensures the recursive feasibility of the learned safety controller. Our method works by constantly reasoning about whether the current information is sufficient to ensure safety or if new measurements under active safe exploration are required to reduce the uncertainty. As a result, our proposed framework can guarantee the forward invariance of the safe set defined by the CBF with high probability, even if it contains a priori unexplored regions. We validate the proposed framework in two numerical simulation experiments.", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b6acea6a8923e550", "type": "tool_result", "content": "Abstract: Commonly used speech corpora inadequately challenge academic and commercial ASR systems. In particular, speech corpora lack metadata needed for detailed analysis and WER measurement. In response, we present Earnings-21, a 39-hour corpus of earnings calls containing entity-dense speech from nine different financial sectors. This corpus is intended to benchmark ASR systems in the wild with special attention towards named entity recognition. We benchmark four commercial ASR models, two internal models built with open-source tools, and an open-source LibriSpeech model and discuss their differences in performance on Earnings-21. Using our recently released fstalign tool, we provide a candid analysis of each model&#39;s recognition capabilities under different partitions. Our analysis finds that ASR accuracy for certain NER categories is poor, presenting a significant impediment to transcript comprehension and usage. Earnings-21 bridges academic and commercial ASR system evaluation and enables further research on entity modeling and WER on real world audio. Comments: Accepted to INTERSPEECH 2021. June 15 2021: Addressing the comments of reviewers and updating the results of our internal ESPNet model. The results do not change our conclusions. April 28th, 2021: We found and resolved an issue in our experimental evaluation that scored the LibriSpeech model at ~20% worse relative WER than the actual WER. The updated results do not affect our conclusions Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Sound (cs.SD); Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS) Cite as: arXiv:2104.11348 [cs.CL] &nbsp; (or arXiv:2104.11348v3 [cs.CL] for this version) &nbsp; https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2104.11348 Focus to learn more arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite Related DOI : https://doi.org/10.21437/Interspeech.2021-1915 Focus to learn more DOI(s) linking to related resources Submission history From: Miguel Del Rio Fernandez [ view email ] [v1] Thu, 22 Apr 2021 23:04:28 UTC (104 KB) [v2] Wed", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "56329db5-c377-41fc-aa2c-a3444e3b2bbc", "uuid": "361175ee-3f69-479b-8967-3aa1a1167e91", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T09:15:54.888Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "Abstract: Commonly used speech corpora inadequately challenge academic and commercial ASR systems. In particular, speech corpora lack metadata needed for detailed analysis and WER measurement. In response, we present Earnings-21, a 39-hour corpus of earnings calls containing entity-dense speech from nine different financial sectors. This corpus is intended to benchmark ASR systems in the wild with special attention towards named entity recognition. We benchmark four commercial ASR models, two internal models built with open-source tools, and an open-source LibriSpeech model and discuss their differences in performance on Earnings-21. Using our recently released fstalign tool, we provide a candid analysis of each model&#39;s recognition capabilities under different partitions. Our analysis finds that ASR accuracy for certain NER categories is poor, presenting a significant impediment to transcript comprehension and usage. Earnings-21 bridges academic and commercial ASR system evaluation and enables further research on entity modeling and WER on real world audio. Comments: Accepted to INTERSPEECH 2021. June 15 2021: Addressing the comments of reviewers and updating the results of our internal ESPNet model. The results do not change our conclusions. April 28th, 2021: We found and resolved an issue in our experimental evaluation that scored the LibriSpeech model at ~20% worse relative WER than the actual WER. The updated results do not affect our conclusions Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Sound (cs.SD); Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS) Cite as: arXiv:2104.11348 [cs.CL] &nbsp; (or arXiv:2104.11348v3 [cs.CL] for this version) &nbsp; https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2104.11348 Focus to learn more arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite Related DOI : https://doi.org/10.21437/Interspeech.2021-1915 Focus to learn more DOI(s) linking to related resources Submission history From: Miguel Del Rio Fernandez [ view email ] [v1] Thu, 22 Apr 2021 23:04:28 UTC (104 KB) [v2] Wed", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
